"""Facing-page guide text for each invocation."""

GUIDES = {

"ganesha-pranama":
"The book opens the way every Hindu ritual opens: with Gaṇeśa, the remover of obstacles. The verse is a meditation instruction as much as a prayer — meditate on him — and the description is a map of the calm mind: white (sattva), moon-hued (reflective), four-armed (the inner faculties gathered), serene-faced (uncontracted). In the Trika reading the obstacle is not only external: it is the contraction of awareness that makes the path seem far away. Say it at the threshold of any work, then pause for one breath before beginning.",

"ganesha-vighna":
"Where the first verse describes, this one asks. The address is intimate and a little comic: a great, curved-trunked body radiant like a million suns. The trunk bends — grace does not travel in straight lines. The request is precise: not make me successful, but make me free of obstacles in every undertaking. Trika would add that the deepest obstacle is the sense of being a separate doer. Say it when you meet the first resistance of the day; ask for the way to open, not for the world to flatten.",

"ganesha-dhyana":
"A dhyāna verse: it gives the mind a form to rest on. Born of the mountain is Pārvatī's son; sun to the lotus is the face that opens the heart. The pivot is ekadanta, the single tusk — many forms for the world, one point for the devotee. The verse quietly teaches what the whole book assumes: multiplicity is not the enemy of attention; it is what attention is made of. Use it as a one-minute gaze practice. Recite it, then let the single point remain.",

"guru-vandana":
"The guru verse is not flattery of a person. It maps the three powers — Brahmā (creation), Viṣṇu (preservation), Maheśvara (dissolution) — onto the one who shows you your own nature. In Trika the guru is the moment consciousness recognises itself through another; the supreme Brahman made visible is not a body but that recognition. Recite it before study or practice, bowing not to a personality but to the transmission that is still happening.",

"sarasvati":
"Sarasvatī is vāgdevī, speech itself. The verse piles up whites — jasmine, moon, snow, pearls — until the mind is washed clean, then gives her the vīṇā (sound) and the boon-staff (grace). The final request is exact: remove jāḍya, the inertness that cannot see. In Trika, speech is not a tool added to consciousness; it is the throb by which consciousness manifests. Recite before writing, speaking, or teaching, and ask for the removal of dullness rather than for more words.",

"gayatri":
"The Gāyatrī is the oldest light-prayer in the tradition. Three worlds are named, then the desirable radiance of the sun, then the request: impel our thoughts. Trika reads the three worlds as the three states of experience, and the sun as the witness that lights all three without being any of them. It is not a petition for things but for the movement of intelligence itself. Recite it at dawn, or before any act that needs clarity, and let the mind be the thing that is lit.",

"purnam":
"The Īśa invocation is a meditation on fullness. That is full, this is full — the source and the world are not two quantities, one of which drains the other. From the full the full proceeds; take the full from the full and the full remains. This is pūrṇāhantā, the full I, the heart of Trika's non-duality. Use it as a frame: say it before practice, and let the arithmetic of loss break. Nothing is diminished by appearing.",

"om-namah-shivaya":
"The pañcākṣara is the most portable Trika prayer. The five syllables are the five acts — creation, preservation, dissolution, concealment, grace — and Oṁ is the triad of will, knowledge and action. So the mantra is not a petition to a distant god; it is the whole cycle of manifestation folded into sound, turning inside the one who repeats it. Say it aloud or mentally: let naḥ open, maḥ hold, śi release, vā hide, ya return. The Name does the work.",

"shivoham":
"Two words, and the whole recognition. Śivo'ham is not a claim to be a deity; it is the confession that the I you just used is not the ego. In Trika, pratyabhijñā is re-cognition: you have always been this, and the mantra removes the forgetting. Say it slowly and notice what says it. If it feels like a lie, that is the contraction speaking — repeat it as a question rather than an assertion, and let the question stay open.",

"soham":
"So'ham is the mantra you cannot stop saying. The breath already makes it: sa on the in-breath, haṃ on the out — haṃsa, the swan. The practice is not to add a mantra but to notice the one already running. Trika calls this ajapa, the unmuttered. Sit for a few minutes and let the breath say it; do not force the syllables. The recognition is that awareness is breathing you, and has been all along.",

"aham-brahmasmi":
"A mahāvākya of the Upaniṣads, read by Lakshmanjoo in the Trika key: the I is not the small self but the full, self-luminous Śiva. Brahman and Śiva are two names for the same recognition. The sentence is not a proposition to believe but a pointer to what is already the case. Say it once, then sit in the silence it leaves. The silence is the commentary; the sentence is only the door.",

"mrityunjaya":
"The Mṛtyuñjaya is a Vedic prayer for release from death — and, in Trika, from the death-contraction of the ego. The image is exact: a cucumber is not torn from the vine but gently freed as it ripens. The last phrase is the surprise: not from immortality — do not release me from the deathless. The prayer asks to be let go into what cannot die. Recite it for the sick, the dying, and one's own fear.",

"karpura-gaura":
"A dhyāna verse for Śiva: camphor-white (pure consciousness), compassion descended (grace taking form), the essence of the world (not its enemy), the serpent worn openly (the coiled power). The final line places him in the heart, with Bhavānī — Śiva and Śakti together, the one heart. Use it as an image to rest in: recite, then feel the white brightness in the chest. It is a prayer of sheer beauty, and beauty is a means of recognition.",

"bhairavastava-1":
"Abhinavagupta opens his hymn by refusing to look for Bhairava anywhere in particular. The Lord pervades every particular state — moving and unmoving — and is consciousness-made, one, endless, beginningless. Then the human turn: refuge of the refugeless. The bow is made with a mind become you — not a body bending but attention consenting. Begin here when the world feels scattered: the many states are the one consciousness in different keys.",

"bhairavastava-5":
"This is the great fearlessness verse. Awareness has already torn the darkness; what remains is not courage but salutation. Abhinavagupta names the ministers of death — Mṛtyu, Yama, the ender, the ghosts of karma — and bows to them, because they too are Śiva's play. The line I never fear is not bravado; it is the natural temperature of recognition. Recite it at night, or when fear arrives. Do not argue with fear — salute it.",

"anuttarastika-1":
"The Anuttarāṣṭikā begins by taking away every method. No contemplation, no speech, no discussion, no visualization, no steadying, no japa. Then the question: what is decided? The answer is the anupāya, the no-means: not renouncer, not possessor — enjoy happiness, staying as you are. This is not licence; it is the end of the search. Say it when you notice yourself trying to become something. The trying is the only distance.",

"anuttarastika-8":
"The last verse of the Anuttarāṣṭikā gathers the whole world into the mirror of consciousness. Real and unreal, eternal and momentary, stained by māyā or pure — all of it is the light of form arising from one's own awareness. The instruction is not to judge the world but to see its shining, and then to enjoy Lordhood. Lordhood is not power over things; it is the taste of seeing them as yourself. A verse for the end of practice.",

"pancashloki-1":
"The Pañcaślokī is a hymn of pure surrender, and this is its first offering. Abhinavagupta does not offer wealth or ritual: he offers his pride. This is my all, he says; apart from this he knows nothing. The request is not for a boon but for acceptance — be pleased. In Trika the last possession is the sense of being the one who offers; when that is offered too, only compassion remains. Recite it when you have nothing left to bring.",

"pancashloki-2":
"The second verse is the prayer of one who has only speech. At the Lord's door the devotee asks that the words victory, victory to Maheśa flash forth faultlessly and unceasingly. Then the real request: make me to have a protector. Śaraṇāgati is not becoming strong; it is being held. Say it at the threshold of the day, or whenever self-reliance has become a burden. The words themselves are the offering.",

"somananda":
"This single verse is the whole Trika in miniature. Śiva enters the form that is myself, and then Śiva bows to Śiva; the obstacle being removed is also Śiva; the power doing the removing is Śiva's own. Nothing is outside the recognition — not even the thing that blocks it. The verse is traditionally linked with Lakshmanjoo's mealtime practice: the bow, the food, and the hunger are one. Recite it when you feel divided; the division is the Lord's play.",

"parapravesika":
"Kṣemarāja opens the Parāprāveśikā, the Entering into the Supreme, by bowing to consciousness itself. Not to a god, but to the heart of the supreme Lord — which is both the world (whose very self is the world) and beyond it. The supreme power is parā-śakti, the highest throb. The verse is a doorway: before any practice, turn to the one who is doing the turning. Say it as an opening invocation, then enter the book.",

"spandakarika-1":
"The Spandakārikā opens with a hymn to the pulse. By the Lord's opening and closing — his blink — the world dissolves and arises. The wheel of powers is the circle of śaktis through which consciousness manifests. To sing it is to join the pulse rather than resist it. Trika's whole practice is to recognise this spanda in one's own breath, thought and perception. Recite it when the world feels solid; let it become a blink.",

"spandasandoha-1":
"Kṣemarāja's Spandasandoha is a condensation of the Spandakārikā, and it opens with a one-line victory song. He alone wins — Śiva, possessor of the pulse, self-established, who makes the inner face taste. The inner face is attention turned back on itself; to taste it is the whole of the practice. This is a verse to say after meditation, when the ordinary world returns: the taste is still there.",

"paratrimshika-1":
"The Parātrīśikā opens as a dialogue: the Goddess asks Bhairava for the great secret — the power that dwells in the heart, the Kaulinī, the leader of the clan. The answer, when it comes, is the anuttara, the unsurpassed. The verse is therefore the question that makes the whole book possible: what is the power in the heart by which fulfilment is reached? Recite it as a question, not a demand; the asking is already the turning.",

"devi-shakti":
"This is the refrain of the Devī Māhātmya, the great hymn to the Goddess: she who dwells in all beings as power. In Trika that power is Śakti — not a goddess beside Śiva but the very throb by which consciousness knows itself. The triple namas is precise: it dissolves the threefold difference of knower, knowing and known. Say it when the world feels inert; the power is already there, in every being, including this one.",

"gita-4-24":
"This is the meal verse Lakshmanjoo taught: water in the palm, then the offering. Read Brahman as Śiva throughout — the offering, the oblation, the fire, the one who offers and the goal are one. The verse turns eating into yajña: food is not taken from the world but offered within it. Say it before food, and let the four Brahmans collapse into one taste. It is the most ordinary and the most complete prayer in the book.",

"gita-15-14":
"The Lord speaks as the digestive fire. This is kriyā-śakti at work in the body: consciousness not only witnesses the meal but cooks it. The verse pairs naturally with the meal offering — one verse for the offering, this one for the digestion. Trika sees the body as the Lord's temple and its processes as his play. Say it after food, or during any ordinary bodily act, and let the ordinary become the location of the sacred.",

"gita-9-26":
"Kṛṣṇa's promise is almost shockingly small: a leaf, a flower, a fruit, water. The value is not in the gift but in the bhakti — the wholeness of the giving. That offering of the pure-hearted I eat. In Trika the gift and the giver are already Śiva, so the offering is never refused. Use it when you have little to give: the smallness is not the point; the devotion is. A verse for everyday generosity.",

"gita-18-66":
"The final instruction of the Gītā: abandon all dharmas and take refuge in the one. This is the anupāya in Kṛṣṇa's voice — when every rule has been set down, only turning remains. I will free you from all sins; do not grieve. The grief is the last obstacle, and the promise addresses it directly. Recite it when the path feels heavy with obligations; the refuge is not another obligation but the end of them.",

"isha-1":
"The Īśa opens with a vision, not a command: all this, whatever moves, is pervaded by the Lord. Then the paradox: enjoy by renunciation. Not possess nothing and be miserable, but possess nothing and enjoy everything, because the whole is already the Lord's. Trika reads tyāga not as giving up the world but as giving up the illusion of ownership. Say it when grasping begins; let the world be the Lord's, and it becomes yours.",

"sst-17-19":
"Utpaladeva's prayer is for the ordinary: not more ritual, but the taste of worship inside every action. Moment by moment is the practice — not a single ecstasy but the repeated return of attention to the nectar of the Lord's worship. The verse asks for the great joy of drinking that nectar in each act. Use it as a walking prayer: at each transition, say it, and let the next action become worship.",

"sst-17-26":
"The body is not renounced here; it is consecrated. Nourished by the clear nectar of devotion rising in the heart, the body becomes fit for worship — and then it is the offering itself. This is the Trika answer to the body-denying traditions: the body is the Lord's instrument, and devotion makes it luminous. Say it in the morning, before the body begins its work; let the day be worship.",

"sst-9-14":
"The sleep prayer. Utpaladeva asks when he will lie down free of every object, settled in undiminished glory, at the two cool lotus feet. Free of every object is not blankness; it is the rest that remains when the mind stops reaching. The cool feet are the peace of Śiva, the coolness after the fever of wanting. Say it in bed, and let the body be the offering it already is.",

"sst-16-8":
"The api-chain refuses to resolve. Crisis or ease, weeping or laughing, grave or unhinged — all of it is allowed. Devotion is not the absence of any of these; it is what holds them. Utpaladeva's prayer is not to become calm but to be devotion through whatever arises. Say it when the day is chaotic; do not wait for composure. The very mess is the place where devotion can be.",

"sst-2-1":
"A salutation to the many-formed one. Fire, moon, sun, Brahmā, Viṣṇu, the moving and unmoving — all are his forms, and all are made of consciousness. The verse does not choose between the many and the one; it names the many and then names their substance. Trika's non-duality is not the erasure of difference but its transfiguration. Say it facing the day's multiplicity; let each thing be a form of the one.",

"sst-2-8":
"A paradox of the recognition: the one who is the sole cause of saṁsāra is also its sole opponent. Bondage and release are the same Lord, seen from two sides. There is no enemy outside to defeat; the world's very form is Śambhu, and it is also beyond the world. Say it when you are tempted to split experience into spiritual and worldly; the split is the only saṁsāra.",

"sst-2-19":
"Every path is his, and no path is. The verse names the right-hand way, the left-hand way, the all-way — and then the wayless. Utpaladeva is not saying all paths are equally true in their rules; he is saying the Lord is not confined by any of them. The final wayless is the anupāya. Say it when you meet someone on a different path, or when your own path feels like a cage.",

"sst-2-25":
"The Name as nectar. The verse piles up supreme nectar — treasure, mass, goal of every goal and of the goal beyond. It is not a word about sweetness; it is the sweetness itself. The goal beyond the goal is the recognition that there is nothing beyond: the seeking ends in what was always here. Say it slowly, as a taste rather than a petition.",

"sst-20-9":
"One bow contains the five acts: creation (delight), preservation (ease), dissolution (the three worlds as food), and the grace hidden in both. The verse is a whole cosmology in a salutation — and it ends with Master, not Lord: the one who is near. Say it at the end of the day, when creation, maintenance and loss have all happened; bow to the one who was doing all three.",

"kayena":
"The closing offering. Whatever I do — with body, speech, mind, senses, intellect, or by the sheer nature of my constitution — all of it is offered to the Supreme. The list is deliberately exhaustive: nothing is left outside. In the non-dual reading, Nārāyaṇa is Śiva, the one who dwells in all. Say it at the end of practice, or at the end of the day; let the whole ledger be handed over.",

"sarve-bhavantu":
"The universal śānti. Four wishes: happiness, health, the vision of what is good, and freedom from sorrow. In the recognition, all is not a crowd outside; it is the one consciousness in many forms. So the prayer is not a polite addition to the personal practice — it is the natural radiation of recognition. Say it at the close, and let the circle widen until it includes what you would rather exclude.",

"om-shanti":
"Three śāntis: for body, speech and mind — or the three worlds, the three powers, the three states. Repetition is not redundancy; it is the sealing of each layer. The book ends where it began, in the simplest possible utterance. Say it three times, slowly, and let the silence after the third be the last page.",
}
